1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:05,050
Welcome to Xmip, the Cross OS Messaging and Integration Platform.

2
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:10,350
Xmip connects your business systems and records what happens to every message it accepts.

3
00:00:10,350 --> 00:00:15,738
It is open source and runs on your own infrastructure, with or without internet access.

4
00:00:15,738 --> 00:00:21,588
We will follow an order through Xmip, then look at configuration, operations, and development.

5
00:00:23,694 --> 00:00:25,988
Different roles need different answers.

6
00:00:25,988 --> 00:00:30,146
For the CEO, it is business continuity when an exchange fails.

7
00:00:30,146 --> 00:00:34,101
For the CIO, it is control over the platform and where it runs.

8
00:00:34,101 --> 00:00:37,731
Developers need a practical way to build integrations.

9
00:00:37,731 --> 00:00:41,937
Operators need to see what happened and recover when something goes wrong.

10
00:00:41,937 --> 00:00:44,246
Xmip brings those views together.

11
00:00:46,334 --> 00:00:50,459
Here is an example: one order, followed through to completion.

12
00:00:50,459 --> 00:00:54,872
These business steps and the thirty-day deadline are choices for this scenario.

13
00:00:54,872 --> 00:01:00,309
The order arrives at a Receive Location, the endpoint where Xmip receives incoming data.

14
00:01:00,309 --> 00:01:05,184
A Subscription checks the message against a filter and selects its destination.

15
00:01:05,184 --> 00:01:12,897
In this example, it starts a Work Process Instance: one execution of the business flow, dedicated to this order.

16
00:01:12,897 --> 00:01:16,759
The Instance tracks the lines ordered, delivered, and accepted.

17
00:01:16,759 --> 00:01:19,334
Every update carries the order number.

18
00:01:19,334 --> 00:01:24,809
Correlation uses that shared identifier to bring each update back to the same Instance.

19
00:01:24,809 --> 00:01:30,147
Once payment is received, or a promise to pay is accepted, fulfillment can begin.

20
00:01:30,147 --> 00:01:34,909
A factory produces the goods, or a warehouse supplies them from stock.

21
00:01:34,909 --> 00:01:39,071
Production updates and shipment notices come back to the same order.

22
00:01:39,072 --> 00:01:40,884
Goods can ship in parts.

23
00:01:40,884 --> 00:01:47,184
The transporter confirms delivery, and the customer accepts, refuses, or returns each part.

24
00:01:47,184 --> 00:01:49,572
The Instance updates its counts.

25
00:01:49,572 --> 00:01:53,559
A refused or returned part must be delivered again or credited.

26
00:01:53,559 --> 00:02:02,409
It waits until every line is accepted, then confirms completion to the ERP, the enterprise resource planning system that manages the order.

27
00:02:02,409 --> 00:02:07,372
If thirty days pass first, the order is marked overdue and the Instance fails.

28
00:02:07,372 --> 00:02:13,272
Operators can inspect its state and values, resolve a failure, and retry or dismiss it.

29
00:02:15,382 --> 00:02:18,771
Acceptance marks the handover of responsibility.

30
00:02:18,771 --> 00:02:25,188
Before acceptance, the incoming stream, the raw data being transferred, remains the sender's responsibility.

31
00:02:25,188 --> 00:02:29,336
If Xmip refuses it, the response explains where and why.

32
00:02:29,336 --> 00:02:34,238
Once accepted, the message is written to storage before processing begins.

33
00:02:34,238 --> 00:02:37,676
Each delivery to a destination is tracked as a Journey.

34
00:02:37,676 --> 00:02:42,231
Its outcome is Completed, Failed, or Dismissed by an operator.

35
00:02:42,231 --> 00:02:44,587
Failed deliveries can be retried.

36
00:02:44,587 --> 00:02:48,831
If no Subscription matches, the message waits in the Dead Message Queue.

37
00:02:48,831 --> 00:02:51,403
Correct the routing, then replay it.

38
00:02:51,403 --> 00:02:53,541
The audit records every step.

39
00:02:55,638 --> 00:03:01,489
Xmip configuration is written in TOML, a plain-text format designed to be easy to read.

40
00:03:01,489 --> 00:03:07,042
One cluster file describes the nodes, routes, and requirements for each piece of work.

41
00:03:07,042 --> 00:03:11,790
Teams can version it, compare changes, and review it alongside their code.

42
00:03:11,790 --> 00:03:17,030
Configuration is validated on save, and each node receives the part it needs.

43
00:03:19,118 --> 00:03:26,743
In Visual Studio Code, usually called VS Code, you can design an integration visually or edit its TOML directly.

44
00:03:26,743 --> 00:03:30,781
Both views work with the same cluster file, so they stay in step.

45
00:03:30,781 --> 00:03:36,768
The Xmip language server provides completion, validation, and renaming across references.

46
00:03:36,768 --> 00:03:43,680
It uses the Language Server Protocol, or LSP: the standard that connects editors to language tools.

47
00:03:43,681 --> 00:03:47,880
Other editors with an LSP client can use those same services.

48
00:03:49,990 --> 00:03:52,703
Xmip assigns work by configuration.

49
00:03:52,703 --> 00:03:54,915
A node is a running Xmip program.

50
00:03:54,915 --> 00:03:57,228
Its name does not decide its role.

51
00:03:57,228 --> 00:04:03,628
In this example, there are receiving nodes, a processing node, an executing node, and sending nodes.

52
00:04:03,628 --> 00:04:07,278
The executing node can receive, process, and send.

53
00:04:07,278 --> 00:04:11,628
Each node declares its roles: receiving, processing, or sending.

54
00:04:11,628 --> 00:04:20,215
It also declares Node capabilities, which describe what its environment provides, such as access through a firewall or an installed module.

55
00:04:20,215 --> 00:04:24,177
Xmip matches those declarations to the requirements of the work.

56
00:04:24,178 --> 00:04:28,465
With no extra requirements, any node with the right role can take it.

57
00:04:28,465 --> 00:04:34,290
You can run all roles on one node, or distribute them as security boundaries and load require.

58
00:04:36,390 --> 00:04:38,395
Work belongs to the cluster.

59
00:04:38,395 --> 00:04:44,381
All nodes use Xmip Storage, and the Ledger keeps track of streams, messages, and journeys.

60
00:04:44,381 --> 00:04:48,997
A claim gives a node responsibility for a piece of work for a limited time.

61
00:04:48,997 --> 00:04:53,241
If a node fails, another can take over when that claim expires.

62
00:04:53,241 --> 00:04:57,422
The primary Storage node also has standbys that follow its writes.

63
00:04:57,422 --> 00:05:00,991
During an orderly shutdown, it hands over to a standby.

64
00:05:00,991 --> 00:05:06,414
If it disappears unexpectedly, takeover waits for the required safety interval.

65
00:05:06,414 --> 00:05:09,634
Xmip does not guess and risk two active primaries.

66
00:05:11,726 --> 00:05:19,751
The same cluster plan supports four deployment models: local processes, containers, virtual machines, and physical machines.

67
00:05:19,751 --> 00:05:23,664
Local processes run as separate programs on one computer.

68
00:05:23,664 --> 00:05:26,576
Containers package the running environment.

69
00:05:26,576 --> 00:05:32,901
Virtual machines provide separate operating systems, while physical machines provide separate hardware.

70
00:05:32,901 --> 00:05:39,339
For virtual machines, the options include Hyper-V, KVM with libvirt, and VMware.

71
00:05:39,339 --> 00:05:44,701
Placement belongs to the environment; the cluster configuration stays the same.

72
00:05:44,701 --> 00:05:48,538
The System Tests exercise the flow as local processes.

73
00:05:48,539 --> 00:05:53,451
The Systems Tests exercise it across Windows and Linux virtual machines.

74
00:05:53,451 --> 00:05:58,001
Each deployment model has its own security and operational trade-offs.

75
00:05:58,001 --> 00:06:00,138
Xmip advises; you choose.

76
00:06:02,254 --> 00:06:04,644
Xmip is designed to run offline.

77
00:06:04,644 --> 00:06:10,113
Build the packages where internet access is available, then bring them into your environment.

78
00:06:10,113 --> 00:06:15,257
Installation, operation, and runtime execution need no internet connection.

79
00:06:15,257 --> 00:06:19,307
The runtime does not download modules or contact a license server.

80
00:06:19,307 --> 00:06:23,682
A node that needs external access must declare the online capability.

81
00:06:23,682 --> 00:06:25,990
Without it, the node stays offline.

82
00:06:28,086 --> 00:06:33,186
Operators can use the Xmip command line, PowerShell, web, desktop, or phone.

83
00:06:33,186 --> 00:06:35,823
All of them read the same operator model.

84
00:06:35,824 --> 00:06:40,561
AI assistants can connect through MCP, the Model Context Protocol.

85
00:06:40,561 --> 00:06:44,874
It provides a standard way for an assistant to use external tools.

86
00:06:44,874 --> 00:06:48,086
In Xmip, that access goes through the command line.

87
00:06:48,086 --> 00:06:53,761
Health is expressed in words: Fine, Paused, Working, Stressed, Exhausted, or Done.

88
00:06:53,761 --> 00:07:00,311
If a group is Holding, it identifies the item that needs attention and shows the evidence behind that status.

89
00:07:02,414 --> 00:07:08,265
Reports present the information Xmip already holds, including audit records and dead messages.

90
00:07:08,265 --> 00:07:12,664
Quests inspect an installation and produce reports from their findings.

91
00:07:12,664 --> 00:07:19,984
The Regulation Quest takes the regulation as a parameter, starting with GDPR, the General Data Protection Regulation.

92
00:07:19,984 --> 00:07:25,189
The same Quest can therefore be directed at a particular set of regulatory requirements.

93
00:07:25,189 --> 00:07:29,998
It shows the evidence, the gaps, and the actions needed for each requirement.

94
00:07:29,998 --> 00:07:32,160
It does not certify compliance.

95
00:07:32,160 --> 00:07:35,261
That assessment remains with your organization.

96
00:07:35,261 --> 00:07:40,213
Firewall, Security, Resilience, and Dependency reports appear in the same list.

97
00:07:40,213 --> 00:07:44,756
There are eleven reports, available across the operator surfaces.

98
00:07:46,870 --> 00:07:49,825
Xmip extensions use a C ABI.

99
00:07:49,825 --> 00:07:56,604
ABI stands for application binary interface: the agreement that lets compiled code call into a module.

100
00:07:56,604 --> 00:08:01,976
Using the C interface allows modules written in different languages to work with the same node.

101
00:08:01,976 --> 00:08:07,854
Work Processes, Transformations, Prepare steps, and other units are loaded as libraries.

102
00:08:07,854 --> 00:08:15,017
You can write them in Rust, C#, C++, or another language that exports the required C functions.

103
00:08:15,017 --> 00:08:18,611
The Xmip Designer generates Rust from a visual design.

104
00:08:18,611 --> 00:08:22,241
Events are notifications about what happened in Xmip.

105
00:08:22,241 --> 00:08:28,166
They use the same interface, with bindings for C, C++, Java, Python, and C#.

106
00:08:28,166 --> 00:08:32,181
Remote clients connect through the HTTP/2 API.

107
00:08:32,181 --> 00:08:36,713
An API is the programming interface a client uses to call the platform.

108
00:08:36,713 --> 00:08:43,190
The connection uses mutual TLS: both sides prove their identity, and the traffic is encrypted.

109
00:08:45,270 --> 00:08:48,720
Xmip is open source, with its core written in Rust.

110
00:08:48,720 --> 00:08:53,995
The platform uses the GNU Affero General Public License, version three or later.

111
00:08:53,995 --> 00:08:57,108
That is AGPL, version three or later.

112
00:08:57,108 --> 00:08:59,770
AGPL is a copyleft license.

113
00:08:59,770 --> 00:09:08,333
Modifications to Xmip remain subject to its source-sharing requirements, including its provisions for modified software used over a network.

114
00:09:08,333 --> 00:09:14,045
The module boundary has a different license: Apache License, version two point zero.

115
00:09:14,045 --> 00:09:19,008
This covers the headers, bindings, and packages used to build a separate module.

116
00:09:19,008 --> 00:09:23,195
Your module can use its own license when it stays within that boundary.

117
00:09:23,195 --> 00:09:27,183
Linking the rest of Xmip brings the AGPL terms into scope.

118
00:09:27,183 --> 00:09:31,445
The distinction is documented in the project's license decisions.

119
00:09:31,445 --> 00:09:38,395
Architectural decisions also record the reasoning behind the platform, and tests check those rules as it changes.

120
00:09:40,502 --> 00:09:45,921
Xmip measures the work it adds around a message, separately from the cost of carrying the data.

121
00:09:45,921 --> 00:09:48,495
Its target is less than a millisecond.

122
00:09:48,495 --> 00:09:52,712
In the recorded test, a stream was carried into and out of one node.

123
00:09:52,712 --> 00:09:57,796
Xmip's own work took a median of four hundred and thirty microseconds across twenty runs.

124
00:09:57,796 --> 00:10:01,149
The storage device's sync time was measured separately.

125
00:10:01,149 --> 00:10:07,411
As of November twenty-eighth, the assembled service includes eighty-six of the eighty-eight declared transports.

126
00:10:07,411 --> 00:10:11,510
Each has been verified carrying data through a node to its destination.

127
00:10:11,510 --> 00:10:17,579
Some transports depend on Linux interfaces, so the available set depends on the operating system.

128
00:10:19,654 --> 00:10:24,004
Xmip brings messaging, configuration, and operations together.

129
00:10:24,004 --> 00:10:26,667
Accepted messages are accounted for.

130
00:10:26,667 --> 00:10:28,754
Configuration is readable.

131
00:10:28,754 --> 00:10:32,342
Work runs where the required capabilities are available.

132
00:10:32,342 --> 00:10:35,742
Operators see the same evidence across their tools.

133
00:10:35,742 --> 00:10:40,579
The getting-started guide walks you through a working integration on your own computer.

134
00:10:40,579 --> 00:10:45,204
The platform is built and continues to develop toward general availability.

135
00:10:45,204 --> 00:10:46,692
Thank you for watching.
